WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.245
Idea. Enzymes are biological catalysts. A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up. Each enzyme is a protein with an active site, a pocket with a special shape. Only a substrate with a matching shape fits. The substrate is the molecule the enzyme acts on. Together they form an enzyme-substrate complex.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.121
Then the products are made and leave. The active site is free again for the next substrate.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:43.927
Method. Step one: identify the cause. A competitive inhibitor has a shape like the substrate. It enters the active site and blocks it. So the substrate cannot bind, and fewer enzyme-substrate complexes form.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:54.569
If you add more substrate, it has a better chance of reaching a free active site. So with a competitive inhibitor, the rate can still reach the normal maximum.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:06.817
A non-competitive inhibitor binds to another part of the enzyme. This changes the shape of the active site, so the substrate cannot fit. Adding more substrate does not help.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:21.596
A mutation is a change in a gene. It can change the order of amino acids in the enzyme. Then the active site has a different shape. The substrate may not fit, so the enzyme works slowly, or not at all.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:37.647
On this made-up graph, the line with no inhibitor rises fastest. With a competitive inhibitor, it rises more slowly, but nearly reaches the same maximum. With a non-competitive inhibitor, it levels off at a lower maximum.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:52.012
Some drugs are inhibitors. Aspirin inhibits an enzyme in the clotting pathway. So the chemicals that make platelets stick together are not released, and fewer clots form. Step four: always finish with the effect.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:02:10.417
Worked example. Now a real exam question. An enzyme makes a gas. A student collects the gas every ten minutes for an hour. The bar chart shows the results. Explain the effect on the results if a competitive inhibitor were added at the start. Two marks.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:23.021
Here are the student's results. Each bar is the gas made in ten minutes. Now add a competitive inhibitor. It has a shape like the substrate, so it enters the active sites and blocks them.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:31.407
So fewer enzyme-substrate complexes form. The reaction is slower, so less gas is made. The bars would be lower.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:37.927
Here is the full answer. It is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:51.891
Common mistake. A common mistake is to say a competitive inhibitor changes the shape of the active site. It does not. It only blocks it. A non-competitive inhibitor changes the shape.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:03:01.621
Another mistake is to say the inhibitor kills the enzyme. Enzymes are not alive. And always finish with the effect, such as less gas.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:17.499
Your turn. Lactase is the enzyme that digests lactose, the sugar in milk. A person has a mutation in the gene for lactase. Explain why this person may not digest lactose well. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:28.245
Answer. The mutation changes the order of amino acids in lactase. So its active site has a different shape. Lactose no longer fits.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.972
So fewer enzyme-substrate complexes form, and less lactose is broken down.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:46.700
So, every time: identify the cause. Say what happens to the active site: blocked, or a new shape. Say fewer complexes form. Then give the effect in the question's own words.
